{"name":"Meander Blue","hex":"#bedbd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meander","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the turns of a winding, crooked, or involved course.","One of a series of regular sinuous curves, bends, loops, turns, or windings in the channel of a river, stream, or other watercourse"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To wind or turn in a course or passage","To be intricate."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meander"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meander","blocks":[{"text":"A meander is one of a series of regular sinuous curves in the channel of a stream. It is produced as a watercourse erodes the sediments of an outer, concave bank and deposits sediments on an inner, convex bank which is typically a point bar. The result of this coupled erosion and sedimentation is the formation of a sinuous course as the channel migrates back and forth across the axis of a floodplain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Meander-en.svg/330px-Meander-en.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meander","wikidata":"Q180537"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/500px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/1280px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG/500px-Rio-cauto-cuba.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Meanders of the Rio Cauto at Guamo Embarcadero, Cuba","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARio-cauto-cuba.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/500px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/1280px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg/500px-Zoltan_Kluger._The_Jordan%2C_near_the_Dead_Sea.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Jordan R